WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.861 --> 00:00:05.993
ISA-95 describes two main flows between level four and level three,

2
00:00:06.108 --> 00:00:08.651
between the ERP and the MES.

3
00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:12.659
Down goes the production schedule: what must be made.

4
00:00:13.406 --> 00:00:17.226
Up goes the production performance: what was really made and used.

5
00:00:17.986 --> 00:00:20.622
In short: orders down, confirmations up.

6
00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:26.406
Master data also goes down, such as bills of materials and routings.

7
00:00:27.166 --> 00:00:30.231
And sometimes an overview of the capacity goes up.

8
00:00:31.521 --> 00:00:32.392
First, down.

9
00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:38.165
The ERP sends the production order to the MES: 1,000 caps,

10
00:00:38.398 --> 00:00:43.402
with 25 grams of granulate per cap according to the bill of materials.

11
00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:50.571
The MES turns it into a work order: the job for one line or machine, in one shift.

12
00:00:51.294 --> 00:00:54.103
Here that is machine four, on the early shift.

13
00:00:55.169 --> 00:00:59.151
And the MES puts the work orders in the right sequence.

14
00:00:59.836 --> 00:01:03.702
For example the white caps first, then grey, then black,

15
00:01:03.842 --> 00:01:07.116
so the machine needs less purging at each colour change.

16
00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:09.901
Then the order starts.

17
00:01:10.627 --> 00:01:15.898
The MES, or the operator on the HMI, sends the recipe to the PLC.

18
00:01:16.881 --> 00:01:20.979
A recipe is the set of settings for that one product: the temperatures,

19
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.733
the speeds and the times.

20
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:26.546
That way nobody has to type them in by hand.

21
00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:28.643
Now, up.

22
00:01:29.372 --> 00:01:31.404
The PLC counts every cycle.

23
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:37.318
The MES collects the counts, the scrap with its reasons, and the downtime.

24
00:01:38.021 --> 00:01:39.670
What is good and what is scrap,

25
00:01:39.879 --> 00:01:45.220
only the shop floor knows: the ERP sees it only when the MES reports it.

26
00:01:46.268 --> 00:01:48.149
At the end of the order or the shift,

27
00:01:48.381 --> 00:01:54.395
the MES sends a confirmation to the ERP: so many good, so many scrap, so many hours.

28
00:01:55.398 --> 00:02:00.460
For this order the MES counted 960 good and 40 scrap.

29
00:02:01.156 --> 00:02:07.681
But in this example it confirms only the 960 good caps: the scrap is left out.

30
00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:15.030
With it, the ERP books a goods receipt: the 960 good caps go into stock.

31
00:02:15.791 --> 00:02:16.906
The scrap does not.

32
00:02:17.977 --> 00:02:23.503
In SAP, for example, the goods receipt for an order is movement type 101.

33
00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:30.084
The material used is booked out with 261: the goods issue to the order.

34
00:02:31.364 --> 00:02:33.210
But how does that material go out?

35
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:38.530
Many companies use backflush: the ERP books it out automatically,

36
00:02:38.646 --> 00:02:41.699
from the bill of materials and the confirmed quantity.

37
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:46.881
So nobody has to scan every bag of granulate that goes to the machine.

38
00:02:47.564 --> 00:02:49.700
That saves a lot of manual work.

39
00:02:50.749 --> 00:02:54.708
But backflush calculates with the standard and with what was confirmed.

40
00:02:55.461 --> 00:02:58.538
If scrap is higher than the bill of materials assumes,

41
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.439
or only the good quantity counts, the stock is no longer right.

42
00:03:03.302 --> 00:03:06.204
In SAP, confirmed scrap is normally included;

43
00:03:06.448 --> 00:03:09.502
the gap then comes from scrap that is never confirmed.

44
00:03:10.897 --> 00:03:12.429
Work it out for this order.

45
00:03:13.144 --> 00:03:16.012
The MES confirmed only the good parts,

46
00:03:16.151 --> 00:03:23.036
so backflush books out 960 times 25 grams: 24 kilos of granulate.

47
00:03:24.058 --> 00:03:29.933
But what was really used is 1,000 times 25 grams: 25 kilos.

48
00:03:30.631 --> 00:03:33.325
The 40 scrapped caps took granulate too.

49
00:03:34.309 --> 00:03:39.220
So after this one order, the ERP shows 1 kilo of granulate too much.

50
00:03:39.943 --> 00:03:41.638
That is how the gap grows.

51
00:03:42.666 --> 00:03:47.460
The fix: confirm the scrap too, so backflush calculates with the total quantity,

52
00:03:47.635 --> 00:03:48.680
good plus scrap.

53
00:03:49.401 --> 00:03:51.874
Or book the scrap separately as consumption.

54
00:03:53.185 --> 00:03:56.645
In practice you hear: stock in SAP is never right.

55
00:03:57.381 --> 00:04:00.806
Often the cause is not in the warehouse, but in the confirmation.

56
00:04:01.827 --> 00:04:03.058
It comes too late.

57
00:04:03.806 --> 00:04:09.274
It is in the wrong unit, for example in pieces where the ERP expects boxes.

58
00:04:09.995 --> 00:04:11.539
Or the scrap is left out.

59
00:04:12.602 --> 00:04:15.714
So ask the sharp question: how often do we confirm,

60
00:04:15.818 --> 00:04:17.676
per part, per shift or per order?

61
00:04:18.401 --> 00:04:19.864
And is scrap confirmed too?

62
00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:25.522
Per part is usually too much for the ERP; per week, too little for the planner.
